INT. FIELD TENT, FORWARD AID STATION - NIGHT

Canvas walls snap in the wind. A single hurricane lamp swings on a hook. Two empty cots. A field radio crackles beside a stack of ration tins.

MEDIC VICTORINE OKONJO-HALVORSEN (30s) sits alone on the nearer cot with her sleeves rolled above the elbows. Her hands are steady. She winds a roll of gauze and does not look at the radio.

It coughs.

BRANDVOLD (V.O.)
(over radio)
Aid station four, this is Control. Report.

She stares at it. She lets it ask twice.

BRANDVOLD (V.O.) (CONT'D)
(over radio)
Victorine. Pick up.

She lifts the handset.

VICTORINE
Control, aid station four. No wounded.

BRANDVOLD (V.O.)
(over radio)
None?

VICTORINE
None left.

A pause on the line. The wind pushes the canvas in and out like breathing.

BRANDVOLD (V.O.)
(over radio)
Evacuation at oh-four-hundred. Pack what you can carry.

She sets down the gauze. She does not move to pack.

VICTORINE
Sergeant. Was it Anselmo?

BRANDVOLD (V.O.)
(over radio)
Yes.

She nods, to no one. She folds the blanket from the second cot into a tight square and holds it in her lap.

BRANDVOLD (V.O.)
(over radio)
Control, out.

The radio goes to static. She switches it off and sits in the lamplight, listening to the wind.
